\card{Definition}{isomorphism, endomorphism, automorphism}{
\label{mk:homomorphism}
\lean{CategoryTheory.Iso,CategoryTheory.End,CategoryTheory.Aut}

\p{Fix the algebraic structures and the homomorphisms under consideration.
An \newvocab{Isomorphism} #{\phi:A\to B} is an \vocabk{iso}{tt-000D}:
a homomorphism admitting an
inverse homomorphism #{\phi^{-1}:B\to A}, so that
#{\phi^{-1}\circ\phi=\operatorname{id}_A} and
#{\phi\circ\phi^{-1}=\operatorname{id}_B}. We write #{A\cong B} when such
an isomorphism exists.
This is the invertible-morphism definition of
\citet{Definition 4.2.4 and the following paragraph, Tag 0013}{stacks2017stacks}.}

\p{An \newvocab{Endomorphism} of #{A} is a homomorphism #{\phi:A\to A}.
The set of all such homomorphisms is denoted #{\operatorname{End}(A)}.
Thus an individual endomorphism satisfies #{\phi\in\operatorname{End}(A)}.}

\p{An \newvocab{Automorphism} of #{A} is an endomorphism that is an
isomorphism. The set of all automorphisms is denoted #{\operatorname{Aut}(A)}.
These notations always refer to the chosen kind of homomorphism; for modules
over #{R}, the notation #{\operatorname{End}_R(M)} specifies #{R}-linear maps.}
}
